Z||000015||California Kid||09-23-2001||11:00 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Sage:<BR><B>A friend of mine who used own and operate a Corvette shop, told me some years ago that he got to drive an 88' Fiero owned by a Pontiac dealer CEO.  He said it had a twin turbo 3800 in it with a five speed.  Claimed that it would have blown the doors off any big block Vette that he had ever driven.  Said the A-arms were tubular and the brake calipers were on the opposite side of the rotors compared to a stock Fiero.  Calif. Kid: Yours is the first mention, (other than my friend), I've ever heard of the "twin turbo" 3800 in a factory Fiero.  He says the car looked stock in every other way, except he said it had "special badging"<P>Any thoughts-knowledge on this?<P><BR>Have a good one!<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>I worked at the GM Tech Ctr during that period and there was a lot of talk about the little giant killer Pontiac was playing with. Can't explain how a Dealer CEO was able to got ahold of a prototype, it may be that he special ordered the car from Pontiac (they were known to do some special orders back in this time frame, which you can't do anymore). Sounds like this car had race suspension parts from IRM, which was working very closely with Pontiac at the time. My gut feeling is that he had strong connections within Pontiac that made him a special car and sold it as a "Stock Model". I wonder if it will surface some day.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000016||Ferrari_cdn||09-24-2001||03:57 AM||||If, and only if, Pontiac is bringing back the Fiero, the Corvette guys isn't going to be very happy.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ferrari_2.JPG">  <BR>'84 2m4, 4 speed, 106k miles (157k km)  AIM: CanadianFiero||||reg||1
Z||000017||Hippocrat||09-24-2001||04:28 AM||||Hey California Kid..<P>I'll be sure to let you know regarding your theory on the big block in the spring. That's our winter project *my brother and I* dropping his 454 into the Fiero.. <P>Personally I like having a frame so I'm sticking with the small block .. still be interesting to see  550 horses in a fiero though.. <P>Anyway sorry for the interruption <BR>I now return you to your originally scheduled topic.. ||||reg||14
Z||000018||Sage||09-24-2001||07:50 AM||||Calif.Kid:  <P>Don't have a clue whar became of this car, but I do appreciate your insights and commentary.  Thanks!  ||||reg||1
Z||000019||gtjeff||09-28-2001||04:39 PM||||Like it or not GM's first rule is to protect the Corvette. The one year life span of the GNX and 1989 Turbo Trans-am are proof of this rule. <BR>One point no one has mentioned about the issue of the never released v8 fiero is that Pontiac stopped making their own v8 engines in about 1980 or 1981. Another division would have had to supply the engine-highly unlikely then. Also explains why we still have no new GTO. Here they are the supposed performance division but arent allowed their own performance engine.<BR>Chevy was totally opposed to the fiero, it has been written about in numerous articles.<P>I work at a gm dealership and one of my former co-workers told me he had heard that pontiac even offered to sell the fiero jointly with chevy, but chevy refused. Who knows if this was the case.<P>The fiero is even today a threat to the present day Corvette. The gt model can record slalom times within 3-4 mph of a 2001 vette. Also fiero gt has a 500 pound weight advantage over a regular 2001 corvette and 275 pounds over the Zo6.<BR>The next generation corvette is rumored to become smaller and lighter-kinda sounds like<BR>the car we all drive.||||reg||1
Z||000020||California Kid||09-28-2001||09:26 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Hippocrat:<BR>[B]Hey California Kid..<P>I'll be sure to let you know regarding your theory on the big block in the spring. That's our winter project *my brother and I* dropping his 454 into the Fiero.. <BR>/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Good luck on your project. A number of big block Fiero's were made back in the late 80's early 90's, some may still be tucked away people garages, waiting for the right time to come out. Pictures below are just a few that were in mags at that time.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/lwf0.gif"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/lwf2_1.gif"> <BR>Sorry about poor quality if pic, old mag.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000021||Formula88||09-28-2001||09:27 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by gtjeff:<BR><B>Like it or not GM's first rule is to protect the Corvette. The one year life span of the GNX and 1989 Turbo Trans-am are proof of this rule. <BR>One point no one has mentioned about the issue of the never released v8 fiero is that Pontiac stopped making their own v8 engines in about 1980 or 1981. Another division would have had to supply the engine-highly unlikely then</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Why is it unlikely for another division to supply V8 engines to Pontiac?  The last Pontiac V8 was sold in 81, after that they used the Chevy (Corporate) V8 engines.  It would be no harder to get the V8 for the Fiero as it was to get the V8 for the Firebird in 82.  <P>BTW, the Grand National (not GNX) was faster than the 'Vette in '87.  I know, because that's why I bought one.  It was all over the car magazines that year.  The GNX was faster still, but ANY '87 GN could whip an 87 Vette in the 1/4 mile.  <P>Rumours of the prototype 89 Fiero GT having a 2.9L Turbo V6 that could eat an 88 Vette for lunch were probably instrumental in the Fiero's demise.  It wasn't money - they made a profit on the Fiero every year they made it, even 88.  They cited a "possibility of loosing $30M dollars" over the next several years as a reason.  This after spending $300M on development?  That argument doesn't wash.<P>If the Fiero was still in production today, it wouldn't be competition for the Vette.  It would be competition for the $80K Acura NSX!<P>||||reg||6
Z||000022||dennis_6||09-28-2001||10:54 PM||||Just more GM stupidity.  It is kinda funny that the Corvette is allowed to survive when cars like the Fiero, Firebird, and Camaro are deemed unprofitable.  <P>More like GM does not like them and tries to make them fail.  Not ever seen a Firebird commercial in all my life.  <P>As for dependable my 97 Malibu caught fire burned to the ground and before that it leaked coolant and brake fluid.  That is a reliable family car.  We don't all want to drive trucks or Japanese like cars.<P>I find it ignorant that some gray hair executive at GM thinks that I would rather drive an import like car than a Firebird.<BR>Maybe we need another American brand that listens to its customers.||||reg||8
Z||000023||Dave Gunsul||09-28-2001||11:06 PM||||I hate to see so many of you believeing the<BR>"it's a small car so it must be weak" theory.<BR>California Kid is absolutely correct, the Fiero was designed to take a lot more power than you think. The original designers wanted a sports car not a eco-box and Pontiac was experimenting with V8 Fieros from the beginning. Take a look inside a V8 <BR>Fieros engine bay and tell me that it's an accident that they fit so well in there.<BR>Archies V8's are actually easyer to change the spark plugs on than the stock V6's are!<BR>Remember the original crash test too, the Fiero came in just behind the Volvo! Is that because of a weak chassis?  I have to hear about how weak the Fiero is from people that have never even ridden in them before and that bothers me. Now I keep reading it in places like this from people that should know better.<BR>BTW have you ever seen a Fiero with a twisted frame from to much power? I haven't<BR>but I sure could take you to look at plenty of Camaros and Mustangs that have and they<BR>were built for it.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||1
Z||000024||Slammed Fiero||09-28-2001||11:38 PM||||A few points of view here (from me)<P>The fiero is built like a sherman tank , the doors feel like the doors on my 79 caddy.<P>After 5 years of autocrossing a Fiero in Street prepared I can tell you you will never ever bend a fiero frame , even if you run it off the track @ 100mph and have it slide on the frame over the chicane , it will only need an alignment ..and you will need new undies (*been there)<P>The Fiero frame won't bend or flex... The A-Arms , tie rod ends and spindles will all on the other hand will warp to hell if you have a really well prepped big bar'd stiff suspended car. <P>It doesn't take a lot of power to keep up with a Vette in a straight line , keep in mind most C4's weigh in at around 3250lbs , Most Fiero's weigh in at 2750-2800Lbs. <P>But , and I don't care what year Fiero  you have it takes a lot of research and development to keep up with a C4 on the track. A set of bars and Koni's on a c4 Do a lot more than the same on a Fiero. <P>How do I come to this conclusion ...5 years of also tracking and autocrossing an 87 Z51. The car is easier to drive hands down. Longer wheel base , better design suspension..ect..ect. Even than 88's sorry to say.  I ran Superstock in Solo 2 and ASP  up here in Canada. The car has since been put on Hiatus in favour of a RS-A 911.<P>Yes a GN / GNX is a fast car in a straight line , but a different marketplace.. Put that GN or GNX on a road course and it would get eaten By a Fiero. Apples and oranges. Even the Mighty GNX had a crude at best rear suspension. Thats not to say it isn't a cool car.<P><BR>Thats my take on things. . the C4 Corvette is a rigid platform  , granted they ride like tanks  but keep in mind in 1984 you could get a Z51 that would break the 1 G barrier on Goodyear  Gatorbacks (ewwww) off the showroom floor.<P>JM <BR>86 GT coilovers , bars , koni's , 11.25" discs , Autocrossed , Tracked Abused.<BR>Co Driver of 87 Z51 , koni'd , guldstrand bars , lowered , Doug nash 4+3 , WC wheels Hoosier Radials , a slew of engine mods. Autocrossed , Tracked , 19,000 track only miles..only driven on weekend (haha) ||||reg||1
